   1:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c **** /**
   2:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * @file
   3:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * Packet buffer management
   4:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  */
   5:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c **** 
   6:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c **** /**
   7:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * @defgroup pbuf Packet buffers (PBUF)
   8:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * @ingroup infrastructure
   9:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  *
  10:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * Packets are built from the pbuf data structure. It supports dynamic
  11:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * memory allocation for packet contents or can reference externally
  12:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * managed packet contents both in RAM and ROM. Quick allocation for
  13:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * incoming packets is provided through pools with fixed sized pbufs.
  14:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  *
  15:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * A packet may span over multiple pbufs, chained as a singly linked
  16:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * list. This is called a "pbuf chain".
  17:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  *
  18:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * Multiple packets may be queued, also using this singly linked list.
  19:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * This is called a "packet queue".
  20:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  *
  21:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * So, a packet queue consists of one or more pbuf chains, each of
  22:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * which consist of one or more pbufs. CURRENTLY, PACKET QUEUES ARE
  23:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * NOT SUPPORTED!!! Use helper structs to queue multiple packets.
  24:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  *
  25:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * The differences between a pbuf chain and a packet queue are very
  26:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * precise but subtle.
  27:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  *
  28:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * The last pbuf of a packet has a ->tot_len field that equals the
  29:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * ->len field. It can be found by traversing the list. If the last
  30:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * pbuf of a packet has a ->next field other than NULL, more packets
  31:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * are on the queue.
  32:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  *
  33:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * Therefore, looping through a pbuf of a single packet, has an
  34:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * loop end condition (tot_len == p->len), NOT (next == NULL).
  35:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  *
  36:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  * Example of custom pbuf usage: @ref zerocopyrx
  37:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c ****  */
  38:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/pbuf.c **** 
